> Verification and references

Native tests compare 10000 full-width random divisions with independent built-in
unsigned quotient/remainder and verify every actual prefix identity in widened
integers, alongside the original examples, zero divisors, maximum values and
capture boundaries. The ten-report batch passed 101 native CTest cases, 34
directly instrumented ASan/UBSan cases, 103 compiled/executed public examples,
515/515 scoped file definitions plus 10 exact LCG class definitions, and ten
representative clangd hovers. Independent Python integer/Fraction,
explicit-label graph search, quadratic sequence DP and full weighted-stump
references passed 69430 records across all ten topics, including a separately
fitted held-out Iris model. Frozen ordinary compatibility passed 1000 Lex-BFS
orders, 1000 library sorts, 100 LBG codebooks, 100 complete five-round
LogitBoost ensembles and 200 LLL bases; corrected LBG final labels/objectives
are intentionally excluded from historical compatibility. All 4 complete
programs executed and all 4 native figures were rendered and visually reviewed,
with clipped legends corrected and rerun. HTML/Markdown computed-result and
figure-byte checks gate legacy retirement. Repeatable receipts are retained in
`build/mepml-conversion/batch-204-213/`.

D. E. Knuth,
[The Art of Computer Programming, Volume2: Seminumerical Algorithms, section4.3.1|https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/the-art-of/9780321635778/ch04a.xhtml],
describes elementary division and its quotient/remainder invariants. The
report’s fixed64-bit cost and captured states describe this concrete
implementation rather than a variable-precision complexity claim.
